gchO(){return"About the same. If it rises again, you can watch it again."},
gchN(){return"Still high. Give it 5 more minutes, the wave is still passing."},
gaN8(){return"Cold on your face for 30 seconds. It triggers a body reflex that slows your heart rate within seconds."},
gaN_(){return"30 seconds, then 1 minute of breathing"},
gaN9(){return"Everything is too much and you can't even breathe calmly."},
gaNa(){return"Your heart is racing."},
gaNb(){return"You need to stop right now, before you say or do something."},
gaN1(){return"Get the water ready"},
gaN0(){return"Fill a bowl or the sink with very cold water, with ice if you have it. An ice pack or something cold wrapped in a cloth also works."},
gaN3(){return'Tap "My water is ready"'},
gaN2(){return"You get 3 seconds to get there. Keep your phone nearby: you don't need to look at it."},
gaN5(){return"Face in the cold, holding your breath"},
gaN4(){return"Take a breath if you can and wet or dip your face, mostly forehead, eyes and cheeks. Halfway the phone vibrates: breathe out slowly and keep the cold on."},
gaN7(){return"Breathe slowly"},
gaN6(){return"When it ends you do a minute of slow breathing so the calm stays."},
gaNc(){return"Cold on your face while holding your breath triggers the diving reflex: your body lowers your heart rate on its own (Ackermann et al., 2023). That this helps you calm down in a bad moment comes mostly from therapists' practice, which is why you finish with a minute of slow breathing."},
gaMZ(){return"If you have a heart condition, a slow pulse or take heart medication, check with a doctor first. If you feel dizzy, stop and breathe normally."},
gaNm(){return"An exercise to come back to where you are using your five senses. You name things you see, touch, hear, smell and taste, from 5 down to 1."},
gaNf(){return"2 or 3 minutes"},
gaNn(){return"Your mind has wandered off and you can't come back."},
gaNo(){return"Everything speeds up or feels unreal."},
gaNp(){return"You have no water or room for anything else: it works anywhere."},
gaNh(){return"5 things you see"},
gaNg(){return"Look around and name them to yourself, no writing. Tap a dot on the screen for each one."},
gaNj(){return"4 you touch and 3 you hear"},
gaNi(){return"Your clothes, the chair, your hands. Then the sounds, even if they are far away or very quiet."},
gaNl(){return"2 you smell and 1 you taste"},
gaNk(){return"If you smell nothing, get close to something or recall a smell. The taste can be what your mouth tastes like now."},
gaNq(){return"To name what's around you, you have to put your attention outside, and that leaves less room for what's spinning inside. Psychologists use it a lot to come back to the present. It has little research of its own, but it's risk free and you can do it anywhere."},
gaML(){return"A colour, a light, something you never look at. Tap a dot for each one."},
gaMO(){return"Your clothes, the chair, the floor under your feet. Tap a dot for each one."},
gaMK(){return"Your breathing, an engine, a distant noise. Tap a dot for each one."},
gaMM(){return"The air, your clothes, something nearby. If you smell nothing, recall a smell. Tap a dot for each one."},
gaMN(){return"What your mouth tastes like now, or a sip of water. Tap the dot."},
gaOd(){return"Put what you feel into one word and say how heavy it is. It seems small, but for many people naming what they feel turns the intensity down."},
gaO6(){return"Less than 1 minute"},
gaOe(){return"You feel a lot and aren't sure what."},
gaOf(){return"Your mood is all over the place and it's hard to think."},
gaOg(){return"You want to understand what's going on before you react."},
gaO8(){return"Pick the word"},
gaO7(){return"Among several emotions, the one that comes closest. There's no right answer."},
