gaO9(){return"From 1 to 10, how heavy it is right now."},
gaOc(){return"See it written"},
gaOb(){return"You see your emotion with its number. If it's heavy, we suggest breathing for a moment."},
gaOh(){return"In lab studies, naming an emotion lowered activity in the part of the brain that reacts to threats (Lieberman et al., 2007). It is called affect labeling."},
gaOy(){return"Talk to yourself by your name, the way you would talk to someone you love. Looking at it from the outside gives you distance and helps you see the situation more calmly."},
gaOr(){return"2 minutes"},
gaOz(){return"Before something that makes you tense: an interview, an exam, a talk."},
gaOA(){return"You are being hard on yourself about something."},
gaOB(){return"You keep going over the same thing and can't find a way out."},
gaOt(){return"Write what's going on"},
gaOs(){return"In one or two sentences, what's making you feel this way."},
gaOv(){return"Talk to yourself by name"},
gaOu(){return'Start with your name and go on as you would write to a friend. Use "you", not "I".'},
gaOx(){return"Read it again"},
gaOw(){return"At the end you see your message. Read it slowly before you move on."},
gaOC(){return"Talking to yourself by name, as if to someone else, creates distance from what you feel. In studies, people who did it before public speaking saw the situation more as a challenge than a threat and felt less nervous (Kross et al., 2014)."},
gaNN(){return"A short pause to treat yourself with the same kindness you would show a friend when something goes wrong."},
gaNG(){return"1 or 2 minutes"},
gaNO(){return"Your streak broke or you didn't do what you wanted."},
gaNP(){return"Something went wrong and you are being hard on yourself."},
gaNQ(){return'You feel like "it\'s ruined anyway" and want to quit.'},
gaNI(){return"Say what happened"},
gaNH(){return"In one line if you want. You can also leave it blank."},
gaNK(){return"Admit it's hard"},
gaNJ(){return"A reminder that it's normal for it to hurt, and that it happens to anyone who tries something."},
gaNM(){return"Choose what you need"},
gaNL(){return"Rest, pick it up tomorrow, a small step or talk to someone. Depending on what you choose, we suggest breathing, starting for 2 minutes or talking to someone."},
gaNR(){return"Being kind to yourself after a slip isn't settling: it lowers self-criticism and rumination. Several studies of self-compassion programs pooled in a meta-analysis show it (Ferrari et al., 2019)."},
gaO1(){return"A few minutes of simple movement, no special clothes or equipment. It clears your head and can lift your energy."},
gaNV(){return"5 or 10 minutes"},
gaO2(){return"You have no energy or feel low."},
gaO3(){return"You have been in the same chair for hours."},
gaO4(){return"Your mind is stuck and you can't move forward."},
gaNX(){return"Choose how long"},
gaNW(){return"5 or 10 minutes. 5 is enough."},
gaNZ(){return"Move however you can"},
gaNY(){return"The screen suggests ideas as you go: walking, stairs, stretching or dancing to a song. Do whichever comes easily."},
gaO0(){return"Notice how you feel"},
gaO_(){return"When it ends, notice whether your energy or mood changed."},
gaO5(){return"A short session of movement usually improves mood and attention for a while (Basso and Suzuki, 2017)."},
gaOO(){return"For that thing you keep putting off. You commit to just two minutes: starting is the hard part, and once you've started, keeping going is much easier."},
gaOF(){return"2 minutes, or more if you want"},
gaOP(){return"There is something you keep leaving for later."},
gaOQ(){return"You sit down to do something and end up on your phone."},
gaOR(){return"The task feels so big you don't know where to begin."},
gaOH(){return"Write the first step"},
gaOG(){return'The smallest possible: "open the document", "take the clothes out". It\'s optional.'},
gaOJ(){return"A 5 second countdown"},
gaOI(){return"Five seconds, so you don't have time to hesitate."},
gaOL(){return"Two minutes doing it"},
gaOK(){return"The timer runs with your first step on screen. You can stop whenever you want."},
gaON(){return"Decide whether to keep going"},
gaOM(){return"When it ends you can add 10 minutes or call it a day. Both are fine."},
gaOS(){return"Procrastination has more to do with avoiding an uncomfortable feeling than with a lack of time (Sirois and Pychyl, 2013). Starting with something tiny lowers that discomfort, and what you've already started is easier to continue."},
gaPr(){return"For that thing spinning in your head. You write it down and answer one question: is there something you can do? Depending on the answer, you leave with a first step or you let it be for now."},
gaPk(){return"2 or 3 minutes"},
gaPs(){return"Something worries you and keeps coming back."},
gaPt(){return"You don't know whether to do something or just wait."},
gaPu(){return"It's hard to focus because of something pending."},
gaPm(){return"Write it down"},
gaPl(){return"However it comes out, no need to tidy it. You can also dictate it."},
gaPo(){return"Is it up to you?"},
gaPn(){return"Choose whether there's something you can do or it isn't up to you."},
gaPq(){return"A first step, or let it go"},
gaPp(){return"If you can do something, you write the first step and we move it to Priorities. If not, you leave it here and can breathe for a moment."},
gaPv(){return"Writing a worry down gets it out of your head and makes it concrete. Separating what's up to you from what isn't is a cornerstone of cognitive therapy: what you can solve becomes a step, and what you can't stops demanding a solution."},
gaNA(){return"Decide today what you'll do at a tough moment you already know. When that moment comes, you don't have to think: the answer is ready."},
gaNt(){return"2 minutes"},
gaNB(){return"There's a moment of the day when you always slip."},
gaNC(){return"You want to keep a habit and you know where it falls apart."},
gaND(){return"The same thing happens every time and you want to change it."},
gaNv(){return"The moment"},
gaNu(){return'The specific situation: "I get home hungry and don\'t feel like cooking".'},
gaNx(){return"What you'll do"},
gaNw(){return'Something small you can do right there: "I make some scrambled eggs".'},
gaNz(){return"Save it"},
gaNy(){return"Your plan is saved on this phone and you see it every time you come in."},
gaNE(){return'Deciding it calmly leaves the answer ready for when willpower runs out. "If this happens, I do that" plans considerably raise the odds of doing what you set out to do, according to a meta-analysis of 94 tests with over 8,000 people (Gollwitzer and Sheeran, 2006).'},
